Product Catalog and Metadata
Product name and description. Every item needs a clear name and a description that appears on transactions and helps staff identify the part quickly.

SKU (Stock Keeping Unit). A unique internal code for tracking each product. This is how the system distinguishes one part from another.

Barcode. A scannable code attached to the product for fast checkout and stock counting.

Category and subcategory. Grouping products by type (e.g., Engine, Brakes, Transmission) so you can filter, report, and organize your catalog.

Brand and Manufacturer. The company that made the part. Essential for a moto parts shop where customers ask for "Honda chain" or "Yamaha brake pads."

Application and Fitment. Which motorcycle model the part fits. This is critical for a moto parts shop. Customers need to know if a sprocket fits a CB400 or a Ninja.

Cost price. What Williams Larry paid for the part. Required for profit calculation and margin reporting.

Sales price. What the customer pays. This is the price used at checkout.

MSRP and RRP. Manufacturer's suggested retail price. Useful as a reference point for pricing decisions.

Quantity on hand. Current stock level. Auto-decrements on every sale.

Reorder point. The stock level at which the system alerts you to reorder. Prevents stockouts on fast-moving parts.

Reorder quantity. How many units to order when restocking.

Location and Bin location. Where the part physically sits in the shop (e.g., "Shelf A1"). Staff need this to find parts quickly.

Product images. One or more images per product. Images appear in the product list, on transactions, and in the POS interface. Recommended specs: minimum 1920x1080 pixels, 16:9 aspect ratio, JPEG or PNG format.

Aliases and Alternative names. Common names customers use that aren't the official product name (e.g., "comp chain" for "Chain Sprocket Kit"). This is what makes the AI search work.

Active and Inactive status. Whether the product is currently sellable. Inactive products don't appear in the POS.

Tags. Custom labels for filtering (e.g., "fast-moving," "seasonal," "Honda-specific").

Serial number and Chassis number tracking. For high-value parts (ECU, carburetor, frame), record the serial number for warranty and repair history. Not needed for screws or washers.

Stock Control
Real-time stock deduction on sale. Every sale immediately subtracts from the product's quantity.

Stock cannot go negative. If stock is 3 and someone tries to sell 5, the sale is rejected.

Low stock alerts. Products at or below reorder point get flagged visually.

Stock movement log. Every stock change (sale, restock, damage, return, adjustment) creates an immutable record with timestamp, user, and reason.

Manual stock adjustment. Ability to correct stock counts with a required reason (e.g., "physical count correction," "damaged item").

Returns and refunds. Process a customer return, add stock back, and create a negative sale record for accounting.

Physical count mode. Enter actual shelf counts, see the difference, and record the adjustment.

Sales and Checkout
Product search at POS. Find products by name, SKU, barcode, or alias.

Cart and basket. Add multiple items to a single sale before checkout.

Quantity selection. Choose how many of each item the customer is buying.

Payment method. Record whether the sale was cash, transfer, card, or credit.

Sale completion. Record the sale, decrement stock, log the movement, and update analytics, all in one transaction.

Sale history. View all past sales with date, products, quantity, total, and profit.

Immutable sales. Once a sale is recorded, it cannot be edited or deleted. Errors are corrected with a new entry (return or adjustment).

Receipt and invoice generation. After each sale, generate a printable or electronically sendable receipt showing transaction details, items, totals, and payment method.

Analytics and Reporting
Today's revenue. Total sales value for the current day.

Today's profit. Revenue minus cost of goods sold for the day.

Week and month revenue and profit. Broader performance view.

Top products by quantity sold. What's moving fastest.

Top brands by quantity sold. Which manufacturers drive your business.

Top categories by quantity sold. Which part types sell most.

Slow movers. Products with no sales in 30, 60, or 90 days. This is cash sitting on the shelf.

Low stock list. All products at or below reorder point.

Profit margin per product. Which products make the most money, not just the most sales.

Demand forecasting. Based on historical sales, the system suggests what to stock and how much. For a moto parts shop, this means stocking more chains and brake pads before rainy season.

Admin and Security
Admin PIN. A numeric PIN that unlocks sensitive actions. Only Williams Larry knows it.

Admin-only actions. Adding products, editing prices, adjusting stock, deleting products, and approving changes all require the PIN.

Approval workflow. When a non-admin wants to change a price or adjust stock, it goes into a pending queue. Admin approves or rejects.

Role separation. Sales staff can sell and view products. They cannot edit prices, delete products, or adjust stock directly.

Audit trail. Every action (who did what, when) is logged. This is how you catch shrinkage and mistakes.

Customers and Loyalty
Customer profiles. Record customer name, phone, motorcycle model, and purchase history. When a customer returns asking "do you still have that chain I bought last time," you can look it up.

Purchase history per customer. See everything a customer has bought, when, and for how much.

Credit and store credit tracking. If Williams Larry allows regulars to buy on credit, the system tracks their outstanding balance and repayment.

Gift cards. Issue and redeem gift cards. The system tracks remaining balance.

Purchasing and Suppliers
Supplier records. Store supplier name, contact, phone, email, and the parts they supply.

Purchase order creation. When stock hits reorder point, generate a purchase order to send to the supplier.

Purchase order receiving. When stock arrives, mark the purchase order as received and update stock automatically.

Bulk import and export. Import product catalog from CSV or Excel. Export sales records for external reconciliation.

User Experience
Product grid for POS. Visual cards showing product name, price, stock badge, and image. Fast for counter use.

Search bar. Natural-language search that uses aliases and AI to find products even when the customer describes them instead of naming them.

Mobile responsive. Works on phone, tablet, and desktop.

Dark theme. Easy on the eyes for long shifts.

Currency formatting. All prices in NGN.

Staff Management
Employee records. If Williams Larry hires sales assistants, record who works when.

Shift clock-in and clock-out. Track when staff start and end their shift.

Sales by employee. See how much each staff member sold.

Commission tracking. If staff earn commission, the system calculates it automatically.